const WORDS=[{"n":1,"w":"bridge","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/brɪdʒ/","def":"A structure built to carry people or vehicles over an obstacle.","ex":"The hikers crossed the wooden bridge over the stream.","es":"puente","org":"Old English","ety":"From Old English 'brycg', a common Germanic word (German 'Brücke'). It first meant a plank or beam laid across water.","triv":"The card game 'bridge' has nothing to do with it - that name came through Russian and Turkish 'biritch'.","trap":"The /dʒ/ sound after a short vowel is spelled -DGE, never -GE: bridge, judge, fudge.","esn":""},{"n":2,"w":"basket","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/ˈbæs.kɪt/","def":"A container made for carrying or holding things.","ex":"We filled the basket with fresh bread.","es":"canasta / cesta","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'bascauda' was a word the Romans borrowed from the Celts of Britain - the poet Martial joked about it in the first century.","triv":"One of the very few English words whose root travelled Celtic to Latin and back again.","trap":"Ends in -ET, not -IT. Say 'BAS-ket' and trust the E.","esn":""},{"n":3,"w":"bright","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/braɪt/","def":"Giving off much light or having a vivid color.","ex":"The bright sun warmed the playground.","es":"brillante","org":"Old English","ety":"Old English 'beorht', from Germanic *berhtaz 'shining'. The GH once spelled a real throat sound, like the ch in Scottish 'loch'.","triv":"That root survives in names: Albert, Robert and Bertha all literally mean 'bright'.","trap":"-IGHT family: bright, light, night, fight, sight. Learn the shape once and you get six words free.","esn":"En español la GH no existe: no la oyes, pero tienes que escribirla."},{"n":4,"w":"careful","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/ˈker.fəl/","def":"Taking care to avoid mistakes, harm, or damage.","ex":"Be careful when you carry the glass.","es":"cuidadoso","org":"Old English","ety":"From Old English 'caru', which meant sorrow or anxiety - so 'careful' first meant 'full of grief'.","triv":"The meaning softened from 'full of sorrow' to 'taking care'. English got gentler.","trap":"The suffix -FUL always has ONE L, even though 'full' has two.","esn":""},{"n":5,"w":"circle","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/ˈsɝː.kəl/","def":"A round shape whose edge is equally distant from its center.","ex":"Draw a circle around the correct answer.","es":"círculo","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'circulus', the diminutive of 'circus' - a little ring.","triv":"So a circus really is just a big circle: the Romans named the arena for its shape.","trap":"Two Cs, two different sounds: /s/ then /k/. C before I is soft, C before L is hard.","esn":""},{"n":6,"w":"discover","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/dɪˈskʌv.ɚ/","def":"To find or learn something for the first time.","ex":"The children hope to discover a hidden trail.","es":"descubrir","org":"Latin","ety":"Old French 'descovrir', from Latin 'dis-' (un-) plus 'cooperire' (to cover). Literally: to un-cover.","triv":"Discovery is not finding something new - it is removing the lid from something that was there all along.","trap":"One S. The DIS- prefix never doubles here.","esn":"Casi idéntico a 'descubrir' - misma lógica, mismo prefijo."},{"n":7,"w":"enough","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/ɪˈnʌf/","def":"As much or as many as needed.","ex":"We have enough chairs for every guest.","es":"suficiente / bastante","org":"Old English","ety":"Old English 'genog', Germanic. The GH is the ghost of a sound English stopped making around 1500.","triv":"GH says /f/ in exactly this small club: enough, rough, tough, laugh, cough.","trap":"Sounds like 'enuff'. Write -OUGH anyway.","esn":""},{"n":8,"w":"friend","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/frend/","def":"A person whom you know, like, and trust.","ex":"My friend helped me study for the quiz.","es":"amigo / amiga","org":"Old English","ety":"Old English 'freond' was the present participle of 'freon', to love - so a friend is literally 'one who is loving'.","triv":"Same family as 'free': in Germanic thought, the free people were the beloved ones of the household.","trap":"FRI-END. A friend sticks with you to the END.","esn":""},{"n":9,"w":"garden","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/ˈɡɑːr.dən/","def":"A piece of ground where flowers, fruit, or vegetables are grown.","ex":"Butterflies visited the school garden.","es":"jardín","org":"French","ety":"Old North French 'gardin', from a Germanic root *gard- meaning 'enclosure'.","triv":"That same root gives us yard, girdle and the -grad in Leningrad. A garden is first of all a fence.","trap":"-EN, like kitchen and wooden.","esn":""},{"n":10,"w":"honest","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/ˈɑː.nɪst/","def":"Truthful and not likely to cheat or deceive.","ex":"The honest student returned the lost wallet.","es":"honesto / sincero","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'honestus' (honorable), from 'honos', honor, arriving through French.","triv":"The H went silent in French and English inherited the silence - but kept the letter.","trap":"Silent H. Also silent in honor, hour and heir.","esn":"En español la H también es muda, así que esta es fácil para ti."},{"n":11,"w":"journey","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/ˈdʒɝː.ni/","def":"An act of traveling from one place to another.","ex":"Their journey across the country took five days.","es":"viaje","org":"French","ety":"Old French 'journee' meant 'a day's worth' - of travel or of work - from Latin 'diurnus', of the day.","triv":"A journey was originally exactly one day long. Its cousins are journal, diary, adjourn and the Spanish 'jornada'.","trap":"-OURNEY. Steal the shape from 'journal'.","esn":"Piensa en 'jornada': misma raíz latina, mismo día de trabajo."},{"n":12,"w":"kitchen","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/ˈkɪtʃ.ən/","def":"A room where food is prepared and cooked.","ex":"A delicious smell came from the kitchen.","es":"cocina","org":"Latin","ety":"Old English 'cycene', borrowed early from Latin 'coquina', a cooking-place, from 'coquere', to cook.","triv":"Kitchen, cook, cuisine, biscuit and 'cocina' are all the same Latin verb wearing different clothes.","trap":"-TCH after a short vowel: kitchen, catch, watch.","esn":""},{"n":13,"w":"library","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/ˈlaɪ.brer.i/","def":"A place where books and other resources are kept for reading or borrowing.","ex":"We researched the project at the library.","es":"biblioteca","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'librarium', a bookcase, from 'liber' - which first meant the inner bark of a tree, the original writing surface.","triv":"English took the Latin root; Spanish took the Greek one (biblion). Same idea, two ancestors.","trap":"LI-BRAR-Y. Say all three syllables or you will drop the second R.","esn":"¡FALSO AMIGO! 'Librería' es bookstore. Library = biblioteca."},{"n":14,"w":"mountain","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/ˈmaʊn.tən/","def":"A very high natural area of land with steep sides.","ex":"Snow covered the top of the mountain.","es":"montaña","org":"French","ety":"Old French 'montaigne', from Latin 'mons, montis'.","triv":"The -ain ending is a French fingerprint: mountain, fountain, captain, curtain.","trap":"-AIN, never -ANE. Unstressed, so it hides.","esn":"'Montaña' te da la raíz; solo cambia la ñ por -ain."},{"n":15,"w":"peaceful","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/ˈpiːs.fəl/","def":"Calm, quiet, and free from disturbance.","ex":"The park felt peaceful early in the morning.","es":"tranquilo / pacífico","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'pax, pacis' through Old French 'pais'.","triv":"The Pacific Ocean was named by Magellan for being, that week, peaceful.","trap":"EA makes /iː/ here. And -FUL still has one L.","esn":""},{"n":16,"w":"question","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/ˈkwes.tʃən/","def":"A sentence or phrase used to ask for information.","ex":"Maya raised her hand to ask a question.","es":"pregunta","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'quaestio', from 'quaerere', to seek. Asking and searching were the same act.","triv":"Quest, inquire, require, conquer and question all come from that one verb.","trap":"Rare bird: -STION sounds /stʃən/, not /ʃən/. Compare nation and question.","esn":""},{"n":17,"w":"whisper","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/ˈwɪs.pɚ/","def":"To speak very softly, using little or no voice.","ex":"Please whisper while the baby is sleeping.","es":"susurrar","org":"Old English","ety":"Old English 'hwisprian' - an imitative word, built from the sound itself.","triv":"Half of English's quiet words are onomatopoeia: whisper, hush, rustle, murmur.","trap":"WH-, and the H is silent in American English.","esn":""},{"n":18,"w":"animal","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/ˈæn.ə.məl/","def":"A living creature that is not a plant.","ex":"The rabbit is a quiet animal.","es":"animal","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'animalis', 'having breath', from 'anima' - breath, soul.","triv":"To the Romans, what made you an animal was that you breathed. Animate, animation and unanimous are relatives.","trap":"Three syllables, three vowels: A-NI-MAL.","esn":""},{"n":19,"w":"birthday","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/ˈbɝːθ.deɪ/","def":"The yearly anniversary of the day a person was born.","ex":"We baked a cake for her birthday.","es":"cumpleaños","org":"Old English","ety":"Old English 'byrth' plus 'daeg'. A plain Germanic compound.","triv":"Spanish describes the action (you 'complete years'); English just names the day.","trap":"-TH in the middle. Do not write 'birthay'.","esn":""},{"n":20,"w":"blanket","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/ˈblæŋ.kɪt/","def":"A large piece of warm cloth used as a covering.","ex":"He pulled the blanket over his shoulders.","es":"manta / cobija","org":"French","ety":"Old French 'blanchet', a white woolen cloth, from 'blanc' - white.","triv":"A blanket was white by definition. A blue blanket was once a contradiction in terms.","trap":"-ET like basket and pocket.","esn":""},{"n":21,"w":"bottle","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/ˈbɑː.t̬əl/","def":"A container with a narrow neck used for liquids.","ex":"Please fill your water bottle before the trip.","es":"botella","org":"French","ety":"Old French 'boteille', from Late Latin 'butticula', a little cask.","triv":"A butler is literally the 'bottle man' - same root, promoted.","trap":"Double T, then -LE.","esn":""},{"n":22,"w":"brother","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/ˈbrʌð.ɚ/","def":"A male sibling.","ex":"Her brother plays on the school team.","es":"hermano","org":"Old English","ety":"Old English 'brothor', from Indo-European *bhrater - one of the most stable words in the whole language family.","triv":"Latin 'frater', Sanskrit 'bhratar', German 'Bruder'. Spanish broke ranks: 'hermano' comes from Latin 'germanus', meaning 'of the same parents'.","trap":"-OTHER, like mother and another.","esn":""},{"n":23,"w":"button","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/ˈbʌt.ən/","def":"A small object used to fasten clothing or operate a device.","ex":"One button was missing from the coat.","es":"botón","org":"French","ety":"Old French 'boton', a bud or knob, from 'boter' - to push or thrust.","triv":"It was named for the pushing. A thousand years later we still push buttons.","trap":"Double T. Compare mutton and cotton.","esn":""},{"n":24,"w":"candle","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/ˈkæn.dəl/","def":"A stick of wax with a wick that gives light when burned.","ex":"They lit a candle during the power outage.","es":"vela","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'candela', from 'candere' - to shine white.","triv":"Roman candidates for office wore bright white togas, which is why we call them candidates. Same root as incandescent.","trap":"-DLE ending: candle, handle, bundle.","esn":""},{"n":25,"w":"classroom","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/ˈklæs.ruːm/","def":"A room where a class is taught.","ex":"The classroom walls displayed student artwork.","es":"salón de clases / aula","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'classis' was a division of the Roman people by wealth, plus Old English 'rum'.","triv":"The top group was 'classicus' - which is why the best books are 'classics' and a good car has 'class'.","trap":"Compound word: double S sits at the seam, class + room.","esn":""},{"n":26,"w":"country","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/ˈkʌn.tri/","def":"A nation with its own government and territory.","ex":"Each student researched a different country.","es":"país","org":"French","ety":"Old French 'contree', from Latin '(terra) contrata' - the land lying opposite, spread out in front of you.","triv":"A country is literally the view: whatever lies over there.","trap":"COUN-TRY. The OU says /ʌ/.","esn":""},{"n":27,"w":"cousin","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/ˈkʌz.ən/","def":"A child of one's aunt or uncle.","ex":"My cousin visits us every summer.","es":"primo / prima","org":"French","ety":"Old French 'cosin', from Latin 'consobrinus' - strictly, your mother's sister's child.","triv":"Latin had separate words for every branch of cousin. English flattened them all into one.","trap":"-SIN at the end, said /zən/.","esn":""},{"n":28,"w":"dance","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/dæns/","def":"To move the body rhythmically to music.","ex":"The children dance when their favorite song begins.","es":"bailar / baile","org":"French","ety":"Old French 'dancier'. Beyond that the trail goes cold - probably Frankish, possibly older.","triv":"One of the few everyday words whose origin genuinely stumps etymologists.","trap":"-CE after N: dance, chance, glance.","esn":""},{"n":29,"w":"dinner","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/ˈdɪn.ɚ/","def":"The main meal of the day, often eaten in the evening.","ex":"Our family ate dinner together.","es":"cena","org":"French","ety":"Old French 'disner', from Vulgar Latin *disiunare - to break one's fast.","triv":"Dinner and breakfast are the same idea. Dinner used to be the first meal of the day, and it drifted later over centuries.","trap":"Double N, like winner and beginner.","esn":""},{"n":30,"w":"early","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/ˈɝː.li/","def":"Before the expected or usual time.","ex":"We arrived early for the contest.","es":"temprano","org":"Old English","ety":"Old English 'aerlice', from 'aer', before - which also gave us the poetic word 'ere'.","triv":"It looks like an -LY adverb but the L was there first.","trap":"EAR- spelling said /ɝː/, like earth and learn.","esn":""},{"n":31,"w":"family","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/ˈfæm.əl.i/","def":"A group of people related to one another.","ex":"Her family cheered from the audience.","es":"familia","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'familia' meant the servants of a household, from 'famulus', a servant.","triv":"For the Romans your family was your staff. Blood relatives were a separate word entirely.","trap":"FAM-I-LY. Do not swallow the middle I.","esn":""},{"n":32,"w":"favorite","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/ˈfeɪ.vɚ.ɪt/","def":"Liked more than others of the same kind.","ex":"Blue is his favorite color.","es":"favorito","org":"Italian","ety":"Italian 'favorito', from Latin 'favor'.","triv":"Noah Webster cut the U from American spelling in the 1800s to simplify it.","trap":"American: FAVORITE. British: favourite. In a US bee, no U.","esn":"Igual que 'favorito' - pero termina en -ITE."},{"n":33,"w":"flower","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/ˈflaʊ.ɚ/","def":"The colorful part of a plant that produces seeds.","ex":"A yellow flower grew beside the path.","es":"flor","org":"French","ety":"Old French 'flour', from Latin 'flos, floris'.","triv":"'Flower' and 'flour' were the same word: flour was the 'flower', meaning the finest part, of the grain. They split in spelling only around 1800.","trap":"-OWER, like tower and power.","esn":""},{"n":34,"w":"forest","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/ˈfɔːr.ɪst/","def":"A large area covered mainly with trees.","ex":"Deer moved quietly through the forest.","es":"bosque","org":"Latin","ety":"Late Latin 'forestis silva' - the woods 'outside', from 'foris', outdoors.","triv":"It began as a legal term for royal hunting land. A forest could legally contain no trees at all.","trap":"One R. Not 'forrest'.","esn":""},{"n":35,"w":"gentle","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/ˈdʒen.təl/","def":"Kind, mild, and not rough.","ex":"Use a gentle voice with the nervous puppy.","es":"suave / amable","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'gentilis', belonging to the same clan, from 'gens', a family.","triv":"Gentle, genteel, gentile, gentleman and jaunty are five different borrowings of one single word.","trap":"-TLE ending, said /təl/.","esn":""},{"n":36,"w":"happy","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/ˈhæp.i/","def":"Feeling or showing pleasure.","ex":"The good news made everyone happy.","es":"feliz","org":"Old Norse","ety":"From 'hap', meaning luck or chance, borrowed from Old Norse 'happ'.","triv":"Happiness originally meant good luck. Its relatives are happen, perhaps, haphazard and mishap.","trap":"Double P before the -Y.","esn":""},{"n":37,"w":"hungry","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/ˈhʌŋ.ɡri/","def":"Feeling a need or desire for food.","ex":"We were hungry after the long hike.","es":"hambriento","org":"Old English","ety":"Old English 'hungrig'.","triv":"English keeps the Germanic H that Spanish 'hambre' turned silent from Latin 'fames'.","trap":"Drop the E from hunger, add -RY.","esn":""},{"n":38,"w":"island","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/ˈaɪ.lənd/","def":"An area of land completely surrounded by water.","ex":"A small island appeared beyond the harbor.","es":"isla","org":"Old English","ety":"Old English 'iegland' - literally 'water-land'. Purely Germanic.","triv":"The S is a fake. Scholars in the 1500s wrongly assumed it came from French 'isle' and inserted an S that had never been there. We have been writing their mistake ever since.","trap":"Silent S - and it is silent because it was never supposed to exist.","esn":""},{"n":39,"w":"laughter","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/ˈlæf.tɚ/","def":"The sound or act of laughing.","ex":"Laughter filled the room after the joke.","es":"risa","org":"Old English","ety":"Old English 'hleahtor', an imitative Germanic word.","triv":"Laugh, laughter, daughter and slaughter all carry that fossil -GH throat sound.","trap":"AUGH says /æf/ here. Laughter has 'slaughter' inside it, minus the S.","esn":""},{"n":40,"w":"little","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/ˈlɪt̬.əl/","def":"Small in size, amount, or degree.","ex":"A little bird landed on the fence.","es":"pequeño","org":"Old English","ety":"Old English 'lytel'.","triv":"English kept both the native 'little' and the French-descended 'petty' from 'petit'.","trap":"Double T, then -LE.","esn":""},{"n":41,"w":"minute","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/ˈmɪn.ɪt/","def":"A unit of time equal to sixty seconds.","ex":"You have one minute to check your answer.","es":"minuto","org":"Latin","ety":"Medieval Latin 'pars minuta prima' - the 'first small part' of an hour. The second small part gave us the second.","triv":"Spell it and you get two words: MIN-it (60 seconds) and my-NOOT (tiny). Same spelling, same origin, different stress.","trap":"-UTE ending. In a bee, ask for the definition - the two meanings sound completely different.","esn":""},{"n":42,"w":"morning","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/ˈmɔːr.nɪŋ/","def":"The early part of the day before noon.","ex":"We practiced spelling every morning.","es":"mañana","org":"Old English","ety":"Built from 'morn' plus -ing, copied from the shape of 'evening'.","triv":"Its homophone 'mourning' is unrelated and comes from grief. Ask the pronouncer for a sentence.","trap":"MORNING has no U. MOURNING does.","esn":""},{"n":43,"w":"neighbor","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/ˈneɪ.bɚ/","def":"A person who lives near another person.","ex":"Our neighbor brought us fresh oranges.","es":"vecino","org":"Old English","ety":"Old English 'neahgebur' - 'near-dweller', from 'neah' (nigh) plus 'gebur' (farmer, dweller).","triv":"That 'bur' also gives us 'boor' and the Dutch 'Boer'. Your neighbor is the nearby farmer.","trap":"American: NEIGHBOR. Silent GH, and EI not IE.","esn":""},{"n":44,"w":"orange","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/ˈɔːr.ɪndʒ/","def":"A round citrus fruit with a bright-colored peel.","ex":"She sliced an orange for a snack.","es":"naranja","org":"Arabic","ety":"Sanskrit 'naranga' to Persian 'narang' to Arabic 'naranj' to Old French 'orenge'.","triv":"English lost the N by mistake: 'a norange' was re-heard as 'an orange'. Spanish kept the N safely in 'naranja'.","trap":"Ends -NGE. And remember: Spanish has the N, English dropped it.","esn":"'Naranja' conserva la N original. El inglés la perdió en el camino."},{"n":45,"w":"picture","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/ˈpɪk.tʃɚ/","def":"An image made by drawing, painting, or photography.","ex":"The picture showed a snowy village.","es":"foto / imagen / cuadro","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'pictura', from 'pingere', to paint.","triv":"Paint, pigment, depict and picturesque are all from that same brush.","trap":"-TURE ending said /tʃɚ/: picture, future, nature.","esn":""},{"n":46,"w":"promise","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/ˈprɑː.mɪs/","def":"A statement that someone will certainly do something.","ex":"I promise to return the book tomorrow.","es":"promesa","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'promissum', 'a thing sent forth', from 'pro-' (forward) plus 'mittere' (to send).","triv":"Mittere is one of English's most productive roots: mission, transmit, permit, submit, missile.","trap":"-ISE, not -IZE. And the S sounds like /s/.","esn":""},{"n":47,"w":"rainbow","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/ˈreɪn.boʊ/","def":"An arc of colors seen when sunlight passes through water droplets.","ex":"A rainbow appeared after the storm.","es":"arcoíris","org":"Old English","ety":"Old English 'regnboga' - rain plus bow, the archer's kind.","triv":"Spanish named it for Iris, the Greek goddess of the rainbow. English named it for a weapon.","trap":"Plain compound: rain + bow.","esn":""},{"n":48,"w":"school","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/skuːl/","def":"A place where students receive education.","ex":"The school will host the spelling bee.","es":"escuela","org":"Greek","ety":"Greek 'schole' meant leisure or spare time. Free time became philosophical discussion, which became the place you discuss, which became school.","triv":"School literally means 'free time'. Tell that to your teacher.","trap":"SCH- is a Greek fingerprint, like scheme and schedule.","esn":"'Escuela' lleva E al inicio; en inglés empieza directo con SCH-."},{"n":49,"w":"teacher","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/ˈtiː.tʃɚ/","def":"A person whose job is to help others learn.","ex":"The teacher explained the new rule.","es":"maestro / profesor","org":"Old English","ety":"Old English 'taecan', to show or point out - closely tied to 'token', a sign.","triv":"To teach was to point at something. The finger came before the lecture.","trap":"EA plus -CHER.","esn":""},{"n":50,"w":"window","lvl":"Basic","ipa":"/ˈwɪn.doʊ/","def":"An opening in a wall or vehicle fitted with glass.","ex":"Sunlight streamed through the window.","es":"ventana","org":"Old Norse","ety":"Old Norse 'vindauga' - literally 'wind-eye', the hole that let the weather look in.","triv":"Beautiful coincidence: Spanish 'ventana' comes from Latin 'ventus', wind. Two languages, two thousand miles apart, both named it after wind.","trap":"-OW ending like shadow and meadow.","esn":"'Ventana' y 'window' comparten la idea: viento / wind."},{"n":51,"w":"accommodate","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/əˈkɑː.mə.deɪt/","def":"To provide enough space or meet the needs of someone or something.","ex":"The hall can accommodate two hundred guests.","es":"alojar / dar cabida a","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'accommodare', from 'ad-' (to) plus 'commodus' (fitting) - itself 'com-' plus 'modus', a measure. To make something fit the measure.","triv":"Routinely voted one of the most misspelled words in English. Both letters double.","trap":"Two Cs and two Ms: a room large enough to accommodate two Cots and two Mattresses.","esn":"¡OJO! 'Acomodar' lleva una C y una M. El inglés dobla las dos."},{"n":52,"w":"achievement","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/əˈtʃiːv.mənt/","def":"Something accomplished through effort, skill, or courage.","ex":"Finishing the science project was a proud achievement.","es":"logro","org":"French","ety":"Old French 'achever', to bring to a head, from the phrase 'venir a chef' - to come to a head.","triv":"Same 'chef' (head) as in chief, chapter and mischief. To achieve is to reach the head of the thing.","trap":"I before E here. ACHIEVE keeps its full E before -MENT.","esn":""},{"n":53,"w":"apparent","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/əˈper.ənt/","def":"Easy to notice or understand; seeming to be true.","ex":"It became apparent that the team needed more time.","es":"aparente / evidente","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'apparere', to appear, from 'ad-' plus 'parere', to come into view.","triv":"It means both 'obvious' and 'seeming but maybe not real' - opposite senses in one word.","trap":"Double P, then -ENT. Remember: a pARENT is appARENT.","esn":""},{"n":54,"w":"calendar","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/ˈkæl.ən.dɚ/","def":"A chart or system that shows the days, weeks, and months of a year.","ex":"Mark the contest date on your calendar.","es":"calendario","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'kalendae', the first day of the Roman month, when debts were called in - from 'calare', to call out.","triv":"The calendar is named after payday. Romans literally shouted the new month into existence.","trap":"Ends -AR, not -ER. Like grammar and grammar's friend, dollar.","esn":"'Calendario' termina en -ARIO, así que la A te ayuda: calendAR."},{"n":55,"w":"committee","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/kəˈmɪt.i/","def":"A group chosen to plan, discuss, or decide something.","ex":"The committee selected the contest judges.","es":"comité","org":"Latin","ety":"From 'commit', Latin 'committere' - to entrust, from 'com-' plus 'mittere', to send.","triv":"A committee is a group you have entrusted something to. Whether they do it is another matter.","trap":"Double M, double T, double E. Three pairs in one word.","esn":"'Comité' no dobla NADA. El inglés dobla TODO. Es la trampa perfecta."},{"n":56,"w":"convenient","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/kənˈviː.ni.ənt/","def":"Easy to use, suitable, or fitting well with a person's plans.","ex":"Online registration is convenient for many families.","es":"conveniente / cómodo","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'convenire', to come together, to suit, from 'com-' plus 'venire', to come.","triv":"Something convenient is literally something that 'comes together' with your plans.","trap":"-IENT ending. Convenience takes -IENCE.","esn":""},{"n":57,"w":"curiosity","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/ˌkjʊr.iˈɑː.sə.t̬i/","def":"A strong desire to know or learn something.","ex":"Her curiosity led her to read about the stars.","es":"curiosidad","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'curiositas', from 'cura' - care, concern.","triv":"To be curious first meant to be careful and attentive. The nosiness came later.","trap":"CURIOS-ITY. Drop the U of 'curious' before -ITY.","esn":""},{"n":58,"w":"definite","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/ˈdef.ən.ət/","def":"Clear, certain, and unlikely to change.","ex":"We need a definite answer by Friday.","es":"definitivo / claro","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'definire', to set limits, from 'de-' plus 'finis' - an end or boundary.","triv":"Something definite has a finish line. Something infinite does not.","trap":"DEFINITE contains FINITE. There is no A anywhere - 'definately' is the classic error.","esn":""},{"n":59,"w":"environment","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/ɪnˈvaɪ.rən.mənt/","def":"The surroundings or conditions in which people, animals, or plants live.","ex":"Recycling helps protect the environment.","es":"medio ambiente / entorno","org":"French","ety":"Old French 'environ', around about, from 'virer' - to turn. Your environment is what turns around you.","triv":"Same 'virer' that gives us veer, swerve and the pirouette in ballet.","trap":"There is an N you cannot hear: envirONment. Say 'en-VI-RON-ment' in your head.","esn":""},{"n":60,"w":"exaggerate","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/ɪɡˈzædʒ.ə.reɪt/","def":"To make something seem larger, better, or worse than it really is.","ex":"Do not exaggerate the size of the tiny fish.","es":"exagerar","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'exaggerare', to heap up, from 'ex-' plus 'agger' - a heap or mound of earth.","triv":"To exaggerate is to pile dirt higher and higher. The metaphor is very literal.","trap":"Double G. Only one word in the pair doubles.","esn":"'Exagerar' lleva UNA G. El inglés lleva DOS: exaGGerate."},{"n":61,"w":"familiar","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/fəˈmɪl.jɚ/","def":"Well known from having been seen, heard, or experienced before.","ex":"The melody sounded familiar to everyone.","es":"conocido / familiar","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'familiaris', of the household - from 'familia'.","triv":"A witch's 'familiar' is the same word: an animal so of-the-household it was part of the family.","trap":"-IAR ending, said /jɚ/.","esn":"¡FALSO AMIGO! En español 'familiar' = de la familia / un pariente. En inglés significa 'conocido, que ya te suena'."},{"n":62,"w":"guarantee","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/ˌɡer.ənˈtiː/","def":"A promise that something will happen or work as expected.","ex":"The store will guarantee the repair for one year.","es":"garantía / garantizar","org":"French","ety":"French 'garantie', built on a Germanic root meaning to protect.","triv":"'Guarantee' and 'warranty' are the same word. French turned Germanic W into GU - which is also why William became Guillaume and war became 'guerre'.","trap":"Starts GUA-, ends -EE. Both halves are strange.","esn":"'Garantía' no lleva U. El inglés sí: gUarantee."},{"n":63,"w":"independent","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/ˌɪn.dɪˈpen.dənt/","def":"Free from outside control or able to act without help.","ex":"The assignment encourages independent thinking.","es":"independiente","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'in-' (not) plus 'dependere' - to hang from. To be independent is to hang from nothing.","triv":"Dependence is a picture: something dangling off something else.","trap":"-ENT, not -ANT. IndependENT, like the DENT in the middle.","esn":""},{"n":64,"w":"necessary","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/ˈnes.ə.ser.i/","def":"Needed in order to achieve a result or purpose.","ex":"A passport is necessary for international travel.","es":"necesario","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'necessarius', from 'necesse' - unavoidable, literally 'not able to withdraw' (ne- plus cedere, to yield).","triv":"Something necessary is something you cannot back away from.","trap":"One C, two Ss. A shirt has one Collar and two Sleeves.","esn":"'Necesario': una C, una S. Inglés: una C, DOS S."},{"n":65,"w":"opportunity","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/ˌɑː.pɚˈtuː.nə.t̬i/","def":"A favorable time or situation for doing something.","ex":"The contest is an opportunity to build confidence.","es":"oportunidad","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'opportunus', from the phrase 'ob portum' - toward the harbor, said of a wind driving a ship safely into port.","triv":"An opportunity is a favorable wind. The Romans took the image from sailing.","trap":"Double P. Then -UNITY - opportunity ends in UNITY.","esn":"'Oportunidad' lleva una P. El inglés lleva dos: oPPortunity."},{"n":66,"w":"privilege","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/ˈprɪv.əl.ɪdʒ/","def":"A special right, advantage, or honor.","ex":"It was a privilege to represent the school.","es":"privilegio","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'privilegium' - a law ('lex, legis') written for one private person ('privus').","triv":"A privilege is literally a private law. The word carries its own critique.","trap":"No D. It is -LEGE (from 'lex'), never 'priviledge'.","esn":""},{"n":67,"w":"rhythm","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/ˈrɪð.əm/","def":"A regular pattern of sounds, beats, or movements.","ex":"The drummer kept a steady rhythm.","es":"ritmo","org":"Greek","ety":"Greek 'rhythmos', measured flow, from 'rhein' - to flow.","triv":"One of the longest English words with no A, E, I, O or U. The Y does all the work.","trap":"Rhythm Helps Your Two Hips Move - the first letter of each word spells it.","esn":"'Ritmo' es fácil; el inglés le agrega la H griega y cambia I por Y."},{"n":68,"w":"abundance","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/əˈbʌn.dəns/","def":"A very large quantity of something.","ex":"The orchard produced an abundance of fruit.","es":"abundancia","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'abundare', to overflow, from 'ab-' plus 'unda' - a wave.","triv":"Abundance is water coming over the top. Same 'unda' as undulate and inundate.","trap":"-ANCE, not -ence.","esn":""},{"n":69,"w":"accidentally","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/ˌæk.səˈden.t̬əl.i/","def":"By chance and without intending to.","ex":"I accidentally left my notebook at home.","es":"accidentalmente / sin querer","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'accidere', to fall upon, from 'ad-' plus 'cadere' - to fall.","triv":"Accident, cadence, cascade, decay and chance all come from 'cadere'. Things that fall.","trap":"Build it in pieces: accidental plus -LY. That gives you the double L.","esn":""},{"n":70,"w":"aggressive","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/əˈɡres.ɪv/","def":"Ready to attack, confront, or compete forcefully.","ex":"The coach warned the players against aggressive behavior.","es":"agresivo","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'aggredi', to step toward or attack, from 'ad-' plus 'gradi' - to step.","triv":"Same 'gradi' as progress, degree and gradual. Aggression is just stepping at someone.","trap":"Double G, double S.","esn":"'Agresivo': una G, una S. Inglés: aGGreSSive. Dobla las dos."},{"n":71,"w":"amateur","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/ˈæm.ə.tɚ/","def":"A person who does an activity for enjoyment rather than as a profession.","ex":"The amateur photographer won a local prize.","es":"aficionado / amateur","org":"French","ety":"French 'amateur', from Latin 'amator' - a lover.","triv":"An amateur does it purely for love. The word only became an insult when money entered sport.","trap":"-EUR ending, a French signature like chauffeur and entrepreneur.","esn":""},{"n":72,"w":"analyze","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/ˈæn.əl.aɪz/","def":"To examine something carefully in order to understand it.","ex":"Students analyze the data before writing conclusions.","es":"analizar","org":"Greek","ety":"Greek 'analyein', to loosen up or undo, from 'ana-' plus 'lyein' - to loosen.","triv":"To analyze is to untie a knot. The Greeks thought of understanding as unpicking.","trap":"American -YZE. British writes -yse. In a US bee, use the Z.","esn":""},{"n":73,"w":"anonymous","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/əˈnɑː.nə.məs/","def":"Having an unknown or unstated name.","ex":"An anonymous donor supplied new library books.","es":"anónimo","org":"Greek","ety":"Greek 'anonymos' - without a name, from 'an-' plus 'onyma', name.","triv":"That 'onyma' is everywhere: synonym, pseudonym, antonym, homonym.","trap":"A-NON-Y-MOUS. The Y sits in the middle, the -OUS at the end.","esn":""},{"n":74,"w":"argument","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/ˈɑːr.ɡjə.mənt/","def":"A disagreement or a set of reasons supporting an idea.","ex":"She presented a clear argument in her essay.","es":"discusión / argumento","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'argumentum', from 'arguere' - to make clear, to prove.","triv":"An argument was originally proof, not a fight. It meant clarity before it meant shouting.","trap":"Argue LOSES its E: argument, never 'arguement'.","esn":"Ojo: 'argumento' en español también es la trama de una película. En inglés eso es 'plot'."},{"n":75,"w":"awkward","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/ˈɑː.kwɚd/","def":"Difficult, uncomfortable, or lacking graceful movement.","ex":"The large package was awkward to carry.","es":"incómodo / torpe","org":"Old Norse","ety":"Old Norse 'afugr', turned the wrong way, plus the English suffix -ward, direction.","triv":"Awkward literally means 'facing backwards'. It is one of the few common words from Norse that kept its oddness.","trap":"Two Ws, and the AWK- is spelled nothing like it sounds.","esn":""},{"n":76,"w":"broccoli","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/ˈbrɑː.kəl.i/","def":"A green vegetable with a thick stalk and branching flower heads.","ex":"Roasted broccoli was served with dinner.","es":"brócoli","org":"Italian","ety":"Italian 'broccoli', the plural of 'broccolo' - a cabbage sprout, from 'brocco', a shoot.","triv":"It is already plural in Italian. One piece is technically a 'broccolo'.","trap":"Double C, single L. The opposite is the usual mistake.","esn":"'Brócoli' lleva una C. Inglés: broCColi, con dos."},{"n":77,"w":"cemetery","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/ˈsem.ə.ter.i/","def":"A place where people are buried.","ex":"The historic cemetery contains many old monuments.","es":"cementerio","org":"Greek","ety":"Greek 'koimeterion', a sleeping place or dormitory, from 'koiman' - to put to sleep.","triv":"A cemetery is literally a dormitory. Early Christians chose the word deliberately, to suggest the dead were only sleeping.","trap":"Three Es and no A: c-E-m-E-t-E-ry.","esn":"'Cementerio' te da las tres E en el mismo lugar. Confía en el español aquí."},{"n":78,"w":"colleague","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/ˈkɑː.liːɡ/","def":"A person with whom one works.","ex":"My colleague reviewed the presentation.","es":"colega","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'collega', one chosen alongside another, from 'com-' plus 'legare' - to depute or choose.","triv":"A colleague is someone selected to serve with you, not merely someone in the same building.","trap":"-EAGUE, exactly like league. Double L in the middle.","esn":""},{"n":79,"w":"competition","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/ˌkɑːm.pəˈtɪʃ.ən/","def":"An event in which people try to win or do better than others.","ex":"The spelling competition begins at noon.","es":"competencia / concurso","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'competere', to strive together, from 'com-' plus 'petere' - to seek.","triv":"The original sense is co-operative: rivals are people seeking the same thing together. Worth remembering at a spelling bee.","trap":"-TITION. Two Ts and two Is in the tail.","esn":""},{"n":80,"w":"correspondence","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/ˌkɔːr.əˈspɑːn.dəns/","def":"Communication by exchanging letters or messages.","ex":"The office keeps a record of official correspondence.","es":"correspondencia","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'com-' (together) plus 'respondere' - to answer back.","triv":"To correspond is to answer each other. Letters were the original protocol.","trap":"Double R, and -ENCE not -ance.","esn":""},{"n":81,"w":"discipline","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/ˈdɪs.ə.plɪn/","def":"Training that develops self-control or a field of study.","ex":"Daily practice requires patience and discipline.","es":"disciplina","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'disciplina', instruction, from 'discipulus' - a pupil, from 'discere', to learn.","triv":"Disciple and discipline are the same word. Discipline originally meant learning, not punishment.","trap":"DIS-CI-PLINE. The SC is two separate sounds.","esn":""},{"n":82,"w":"embarrass","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/ɪmˈber.əs/","def":"To make someone feel self-conscious or ashamed.","ex":"A kind teammate would never embarrass a new player.","es":"avergonzar","org":"Spanish","ety":"Spanish and Portuguese 'embarazar', to obstruct, from 'barra' - a bar. To embarrass was to put a bar in someone's path.","triv":"English borrowed this one from Spanish, through French. You are looking at your own language in a mirror.","trap":"Double R and double S. Two pairs.","esn":"¡EL FALSO AMIGO MÁS FAMOSO! 'Embarazada' = pregnant. 'Embarrassed' = avergonzado. Nunca los confundas."},{"n":83,"w":"existence","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/ɪɡˈzɪs.təns/","def":"The state of being real or alive.","ex":"The discovery proved the existence of the ancient settlement.","es":"existencia","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'existere', to step forth or emerge, from 'ex-' plus 'sistere' - to stand.","triv":"To exist is to stand out from nothing. The Romans made it sound like an act of nerve.","trap":"-ENCE, not -ance. And the E after T: exist-E-nce.","esn":""},{"n":84,"w":"foreign","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/ˈfɔːr.ən/","def":"Coming from or relating to another country.","ex":"She enjoys learning foreign languages.","es":"extranjero","org":"Latin","ety":"Old French 'forain', from Latin 'foranus' - on the outside, from 'foris', outdoors. The same 'foris' that gave us forest.","triv":"The G is fake. It was inserted in the 1500s by analogy with 'reign' and 'sovereign' - the same fashion for tidy-looking Latin that put the S in island.","trap":"Silent G, and EI not IE. A doubly dishonest word.","esn":""},{"n":85,"w":"government","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/ˈɡʌv.ɚn.mənt/","def":"The system or group that governs a country or community.","ex":"The local government approved the new park.","es":"gobierno","org":"Greek","ety":"Latin 'gubernare', to steer a ship, borrowed from Greek 'kybernan'.","triv":"To govern is to steer. 'Cybernetics' and 'cyberspace' come from that very same Greek verb - so government and cyber are cousins.","trap":"The hidden N: goverNment. Americans rarely pronounce it, and then misspell it.","esn":""},{"n":86,"w":"grammar","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/ˈɡræm.ɚ/","def":"The rules that govern how words form sentences.","ex":"Good grammar makes writing easier to understand.","es":"gramática","org":"Greek","ety":"Greek 'grammatike', the art of letters, from 'gramma' - a letter.","triv":"'Glamour' is the same word. In Scots, 'grammar' meant learning, then occult learning, then magic - which drifted into charm and beauty.","trap":"Ends -AR, like calendar. Not 'grammer'.","esn":""},{"n":87,"w":"interrupt","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/ˌɪn.t̬əˈrʌpt/","def":"To stop someone or something briefly before it is finished.","ex":"Please do not interrupt while another student is speaking.","es":"interrumpir","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'interrumpere', to break between, from 'inter-' plus 'rumpere' - to break.","triv":"Same 'rumpere' as rupture, erupt, abrupt and bankrupt.","trap":"Double R.","esn":"Buenas noticias: 'interrumpir' también lleva doble R. Esta sí coincide."},{"n":88,"w":"knowledge","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/ˈnɑː.lɪdʒ/","def":"Information and understanding gained through learning or experience.","ex":"Reading expands our knowledge of the world.","es":"conocimiento","org":"Old English","ety":"Middle English 'knowleche' - 'know' plus an old suffix marking action or condition.","triv":"The silent K was pronounced until about 1600. Shakespeare's audience said 'k-now'.","trap":"Silent K, and the tail is -LEDGE, like the edge of a cliff.","esn":""},{"n":89,"w":"maintenance","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/ˈmeɪn.tən.əns/","def":"Work done to keep something in good condition.","ex":"Regular maintenance keeps the machine running safely.","es":"mantenimiento","org":"Latin","ety":"Old French 'maintenir', from Latin 'manu tenere' - to hold in the hand.","triv":"Maintenance is literally keeping a grip on something.","trap":"The cruel one: maintAIN becomes maintEN-ance. The vowel changes when the suffix arrives.","esn":""},{"n":90,"w":"maneuver","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/məˈnuː.vɚ/","def":"A careful or skillful movement.","ex":"The driver completed a difficult maneuver.","es":"maniobra","org":"French","ety":"French 'manoeuvre', from Latin 'manu operari' - to work by hand.","triv":"'Manure' comes from the same phrase: land that was worked by hand. A maneuver and manure are etymological twins.","trap":"American MANEUVER. British writes manoeuvre. Use the American form.","esn":"'Maniobra' = mano + obra. El inglés dice lo mismo: man + oeuvre."},{"n":91,"w":"miniature","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/ˈmɪn.i.ə.tʃɚ/","def":"Much smaller than the usual size.","ex":"The museum displayed a miniature model of the city.","es":"miniatura","org":"Italian","ety":"Italian 'miniatura', from 'minium' - the red lead pigment used to decorate medieval manuscripts.","triv":"It had nothing to do with smallness. It meant a manuscript painting, and because those paintings were tiny, the sound of 'mini-' hijacked the meaning.","trap":"MIN-I-A-TURE. Four syllables. Do not lose the A.","esn":""},{"n":92,"w":"mischievous","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/ˈmɪs.tʃə.vəs/","def":"Playfully causing minor trouble.","ex":"The mischievous kitten hid under the sofa.","es":"travieso","org":"French","ety":"Old French 'meschever', to come to grief, from 'mes-' (badly) plus 'chief' (head) - the same 'chief' as in achievement.","triv":"Achievement is coming to a good head; mischief is coming to a bad one.","trap":"THREE syllables: MIS-chie-vous. There is no I before the -OUS. 'Mischievious' is not a word.","esn":""},{"n":93,"w":"occasionally","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/əˈkeɪ.ʒən.əl.i/","def":"Sometimes, but not regularly or often.","ex":"We occasionally practice outdoors.","es":"ocasionalmente / de vez en cuando","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'occasio', a falling toward or opportunity, from 'ob-' plus 'cadere' - to fall.","triv":"An occasion is something that falls your way - the same 'fall' as in accident.","trap":"Double C, single S, then -ALLY. Two Cs, one S, two Ls.","esn":""},{"n":94,"w":"perseverance","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/ˌpɝː.səˈvɪr.əns/","def":"Continued effort despite difficulty or delay.","ex":"Her perseverance helped her master the difficult list.","es":"perseverancia","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'perseverare', to persist, from 'per-' (thoroughly) plus 'severus' - strict, serious.","triv":"To persevere is to be relentlessly serious about something. NASA named a Mars rover after it.","trap":"-ANCE, not -ence. Perseverance ends like abundance.","esn":""},{"n":95,"w":"possession","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/pəˈzeʃ.ən/","def":"Something owned or the state of owning something.","ex":"The medal became his most treasured possession.","es":"posesión","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'possidere', to possess, from 'potis' (able, powerful) plus 'sedere' - to sit. To own is to sit as master of.","triv":"Ownership in Latin was literally about who gets the chair.","trap":"Four Ss in two pairs: po-SS-e-SS-ion.","esn":"'Posesión' lleva S sencillas. Inglés: poSSeSSion. Dobla las dos."},{"n":96,"w":"recommend","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/ˌrek.əˈmend/","def":"To suggest something as suitable or worthwhile.","ex":"I recommend reading the sentence aloud.","es":"recomendar","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 're-' plus 'commendare', to entrust, from 'com-' plus 'mandare' - to commit to someone's charge.","triv":"To recommend is to hand something over for safekeeping a second time.","trap":"One C, two Ms. It is RE plus COMMEND - and 'commend' is where the Ms come from.","esn":"'Recomendar': una M. Inglés: recoMMend, dos M. Pero solo UNA C."},{"n":97,"w":"restaurant","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/ˈres.tə.rɑːnt/","def":"A place where meals are prepared and served to customers.","ex":"The restaurant specializes in regional dishes.","es":"restaurante","org":"French","ety":"French, the present participle of 'restaurer' - to restore.","triv":"The first Paris establishments to use the name sold restorative meat broths to the unwell. The soup named the building.","trap":"-AURANT. The middle is unstressed and easy to lose: rest-AU-rant.","esn":""},{"n":98,"w":"separate","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/ˈsep.ɚ.ət/","def":"Existing or kept apart from something else.","ex":"Keep the answer sheet in a separate folder.","es":"separar / separado","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'separare', from 'se-' (apart) plus 'parare' - to prepare or make ready.","triv":"Consistently among the top five misspelled words in English. The culprit is the unstressed middle A.","trap":"There is A RAT in sepARATe. Never 'seperate'.","esn":""},{"n":99,"w":"successful","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/səkˈses.fəl/","def":"Achieving the result that was wanted or intended.","ex":"Careful planning made the event successful.","es":"exitoso","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'succedere', to come after or go up under, from 'sub-' plus 'cedere' - to go.","triv":"Success originally meant simply 'what follows' - an outcome, good or bad. It turned positive over time.","trap":"Double C, double S, single L in -FUL.","esn":"¡FALSO AMIGO! 'Suceso' = an event. Success = éxito. Y 'exit' NO es éxito, es 'salida'."},{"n":100,"w":"temperature","lvl":"Intermediate","ipa":"/ˈtem.prə.tʃɚ/","def":"A measure of how hot or cold something is.","ex":"The temperature dropped during the night.","es":"temperatura","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'temperatura', from 'temperare' - to mix in due proportion, to moderate.","triv":"Temper, temperament, temperate and tempera paint all come from the same idea of correct mixing.","trap":"TEM-PER-A-TURE. Americans swallow the -PER-, then forget to write it.","esn":""},{"n":101,"w":"acquiesce","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/ˌæk.wiˈes/","def":"To accept something reluctantly but without protest.","ex":"The council refused to acquiesce to the unreasonable demand.","es":"consentir / acceder","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'acquiescere', to come to rest, from 'ad-' plus 'quiescere' - to be quiet.","triv":"To acquiesce is to go quiet. Agreement, in Latin, was the sound of someone stopping their objection.","trap":"CQ in the middle, -SCE at the end. Two of English's rarest letter clusters in one short word.","esn":""},{"n":102,"w":"bellwether","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/ˈbelˌweð.ɚ/","def":"A person or thing that indicates a future trend.","ex":"Analysts viewed the election as a bellwether for public opinion.","es":"indicador / señal anticipada","org":"Old English","ety":"A 'wether' is a castrated ram. Shepherds hung a bell on the lead wether so the flock could be tracked by ear.","triv":"The whole flock follows the bell. That is why a bellwether state predicts an election.","trap":"WETHER - not 'weather', not 'whether'. Three homophones, and this is the sheep one.","esn":""},{"n":103,"w":"bourgeois","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/ˈbʊr.ʒwɑː/","def":"Relating to the middle class, often with an emphasis on material comfort.","ex":"The novel satirizes the manners of bourgeois society.","es":"burgués","org":"French","ety":"French 'bourgeois', a town-dweller, from 'bourg' - a town, borrowed from Germanic 'burg', a fortified place.","triv":"Same 'burg' as in Hamburg, Edinburgh and 'borough'. A bourgeois is simply a townie, until Marx got hold of the word.","trap":"-GEOIS says /ʒwɑː/. Five letters, almost none of them doing what you expect.","esn":""},{"n":104,"w":"chiaroscuro","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/kiˌɑː.rəˈskʊr.oʊ/","def":"The artistic use of strong contrasts between light and dark.","ex":"The painter used chiaroscuro to give the portrait dramatic depth.","es":"claroscuro","org":"Italian","ety":"Italian 'chiaro' (light, clear) plus 'oscuro' (dark) - the art of strong light-dark contrast, perfected by Caravaggio.","triv":"Spanish has the identical compound: claroscuro. You already know this word, just with Italian spelling.","trap":"CHIA- is said /kjɑː/. The H is Italian, marking a hard C.","esn":"'Claroscuro' = chiaro + oscuro. Misma palabra, ortografía italiana."},{"n":105,"w":"connoisseur","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/ˌkɑː.nəˈsɝː/","def":"A person with expert knowledge and refined judgment in a particular field.","ex":"A tea connoisseur explained the subtle flavors.","es":"conocedor / entendido","org":"French","ety":"Old French 'connoisseur', one who knows, from 'connoistre' (modern 'connaitre'), from Latin 'cognoscere' - to get to know.","triv":"French itself respelled the word 'connaisseur' in 1835. English kept the older spelling, so the English form is now more archaic than the French one.","trap":"Double N, double S, and the French -EUR ending. CONNOI-SSEUR.","esn":"Misma raiz latina que 'conocer' y 'conocedor': cognoscere."},{"n":106,"w":"deleterious","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/ˌdel.əˈtɪr.i.əs/","def":"Causing harm or damage.","ex":"Prolonged drought had a deleterious effect on the crops.","es":"nocivo / perjudicial","org":"Greek","ety":"Greek 'deleterios', destructive, from 'deleisthai' - to hurt.","triv":"It sounds mild and bureaucratic, which is why it turns up so often in warnings about things that are genuinely harmful.","trap":"DELETE plus -RIOUS. The word 'delete' is hiding in front.","esn":""},{"n":107,"w":"effervescent","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/ˌef.ɚˈves.ənt/","def":"Bubbly, lively, and enthusiastic.","ex":"Her effervescent personality energized the entire room.","es":"efervescente","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'effervescere', to boil up and out, from 'ex-' plus 'fervere' - to boil.","triv":"Same 'fervere' as fervent and fervor. A bubbly personality and a boiling kettle are the same metaphor.","trap":"Double F, then -ESCENT. That -escent suffix means 'beginning to' - as in adolescent and luminescent.","esn":""},{"n":108,"w":"ephemeral","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/ɪˈfem.ɚ.əl/","def":"Lasting for only a very short time.","ex":"The artist captured the ephemeral beauty of morning mist.","es":"efímero","org":"Greek","ety":"Greek 'ephemeros', lasting only a day, from 'epi-' (on) plus 'hemera' - day.","triv":"Mayflies are classified as Ephemeroptera - 'day-winged'. Some adults live a matter of hours.","trap":"PH for /f/, and the tail is -ERAL.","esn":"'Efímero' usa F; el inglés usa PH. Esa es toda la diferencia."},{"n":109,"w":"idiosyncrasy","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/ˌɪd.i.əˈsɪŋ.krə.si/","def":"A distinctive habit or feature of a person or thing.","ex":"His habit of organizing books by color was a harmless idiosyncrasy.","es":"idiosincrasia / peculiaridad","org":"Greek","ety":"Greek 'idios' (one's own) plus 'syn' (together) plus 'krasis' (a mixing) - your own particular blend of the bodily humors.","triv":"It comes from ancient medicine: your personality was literally your private mixture of fluids.","trap":"Ends -SY, not -CY. Only a tiny club does this: idiosyncrasy, hypocrisy, apostasy.","esn":""},{"n":110,"w":"indefatigable","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/ˌɪn.dɪˈfæt.ɪ.ɡə.bəl/","def":"Able to continue for a long time without becoming tired.","ex":"The indefatigable researcher checked every source twice.","es":"incansable","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'in-' (not) plus 'defatigare' - to tire out completely, from 'fatigare', to weary.","triv":"Six syllables to say 'never tired'. The word itself refuses to stop.","trap":"Built in blocks: IN + DE + FATIG + ABLE. 'Fatigue' is the core, minus its U.","esn":""},{"n":111,"w":"liaison","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/liˈeɪ.zɑːn/","def":"Communication or cooperation between people or groups.","ex":"She served as the liaison between the school and the community.","es":"enlace / vínculo","org":"French","ety":"French 'liaison', a binding, from 'lier' - to bind, from Latin 'ligare'.","triv":"It started as a cooking term for thickening a sauce. Same root as ligament, oblige and rely.","trap":"Two Is, and they are not together: li-AI-son. Watch the middle.","esn":""},{"n":112,"w":"mnemonic","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/nɪˈmɑː.nɪk/","def":"A device or pattern used to help the memory.","ex":"The class invented a mnemonic for the order of the planets.","es":"mnemotécnico / regla para recordar","org":"Greek","ety":"Greek 'mnemonikos', from 'mnemon' (mindful) - and behind that Mnemosyne, the Titan goddess of memory and mother of the nine Muses.","triv":"The word for a memory trick is itself famously hard to remember. It begins with a silent M.","trap":"Silent M at the start: M-N-E-. If you hear /nɪˈmɒnɪk/, write MN.","esn":""},{"n":113,"w":"onomatopoeia","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/ˌɑː.nəˌmæt.əˈpiː.ə/","def":"The formation of a word that imitates the sound it describes.","ex":"Buzz is an example of onomatopoeia.","es":"onomatopeya","org":"Greek","ety":"Greek 'onoma' (name) plus 'poiein' (to make) - name-making. The same 'poiein' gives us poet: a maker.","triv":"A word that means 'making words out of sounds', built out of a Greek word for making things.","trap":"The ending is -POEIA. Say it in chunks: ON-O-MAT-O-PE-IA.","esn":"'Onomatopeya' termina en -peya. El inglés escribe -poeia. Ahí está la trampa."},{"n":114,"w":"perspicacious","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/ˌpɝː.spɪˈkeɪ.ʃəs/","def":"Having a keen ability to notice and understand things.","ex":"The perspicacious editor spotted the weakness in the argument.","es":"perspicaz","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'perspicax', sharp-sighted, from 'perspicere' - to see through, 'per-' plus 'specere', to look.","triv":"Do not confuse it with 'perspicuous', which means clear to understand. One is the sharp eye, the other is the clear thing.","trap":"-ACIOUS, like audacious and tenacious.","esn":"'Perspicaz' es la misma palabra, más corta. El inglés añade -ious."},{"n":115,"w":"quintessential","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/ˌkwɪn.tɪˈsen.ʃəl/","def":"Representing the most perfect or typical example of something.","ex":"The small bakery is the quintessential neighborhood business.","es":"por excelencia / quintaesencial","org":"Latin","ety":"Medieval Latin 'quinta essentia' - the fifth essence. Beyond earth, water, air and fire, alchemists believed in a fifth, perfect element that made up the heavens.","triv":"To call something quintessential is to say it is made of the stuff of the stars. Best compliment in English.","trap":"Double S in the middle, -TIAL at the end.","esn":""},{"n":116,"w":"sesquipedalian","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/ˌses.kwɪ.pɪˈdeɪ.li.ən/","def":"Characterized by the use of long words.","ex":"The speaker's sesquipedalian style amused the language club.","es":"rimbombante / de palabras largas","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'sesquipedalis' - a foot and a half long, from 'sesqui-' (one and a half) plus 'pes, pedis' (foot). Horace used it in his Ars Poetica to mock poets who used 'words a foot and a half long'.","triv":"It is a very long word that means 'using very long words'. English's finest self-referential joke, and it is two thousand years old.","trap":"SESQUI-PED-ALIAN. The QU is silent-looking but says /kw/.","esn":""},{"n":117,"w":"anachronism","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/əˈnæk.rə.nɪ.zəm/","def":"Something placed in a historical time where it does not belong.","ex":"A wristwatch in the medieval film would be an anachronism.","es":"anacronismo","org":"Greek","ety":"Greek 'ana-' (against, back) plus 'chronos' (time) - something set against its own time.","triv":"Shakespeare put a striking clock in Julius Caesar. Mechanical clocks were fourteen centuries away.","trap":"CHR- for /kr/, a Greek signature like chronic and chorus.","esn":""},{"n":118,"w":"antediluvian","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/ˌæn.ti.dɪˈluː.vi.ən/","def":"Extremely old-fashioned or ancient.","ex":"The office finally replaced its antediluvian filing system.","es":"antediluviano","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'ante' (before) plus 'diluvium' (flood) - literally, from before Noah's Flood.","triv":"It was a serious geological term before it became a joke about your uncle's opinions.","trap":"ANTE-, not ANTI-. 'Ante' means before; 'anti' means against. Different words entirely.","esn":""},{"n":119,"w":"apocryphal","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/əˈpɑː.krə.fəl/","def":"Widely repeated but probably not true.","ex":"The apocryphal story has never been supported by evidence.","es":"apócrifo","org":"Greek","ety":"Greek 'apokryphos', hidden away, from 'apo-' plus 'kryptein' - to hide.","triv":"Same 'kryptein' as crypt, cryptic and cryptography. The Apocrypha were the books kept hidden from the canon, so 'hidden' came to mean 'of doubtful truth'.","trap":"PH for /f/, and a Y where you want an I: apoCRYphal.","esn":""},{"n":120,"w":"assiduous","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/əˈsɪdʒ.u.əs/","def":"Showing great care, attention, and persistent effort.","ex":"Her assiduous preparation impressed the judges.","es":"aplicado / constante","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'assiduus', from 'ad-' plus 'sedere' - to sit. One who sits down to the task and stays there.","triv":"The Romans measured diligence by who was still in the chair.","trap":"Double S, then -UOUS. Three vowels in a row at the end.","esn":""},{"n":121,"w":"cacophony","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/kəˈkɑː.fə.ni/","def":"A harsh mixture of loud, unpleasant sounds.","ex":"A cacophony of horns echoed through the street.","es":"cacofonía","org":"Greek","ety":"Greek 'kakos' (bad) plus 'phone' (sound).","triv":"Its opposite, euphony, uses 'eu-' (good) - the same 'eu' as in euphoria and eulogy.","trap":"Starts with CAC-, PH for /f/.","esn":""},{"n":122,"w":"circumlocution","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/ˌsɝː.kəm.loʊˈkjuː.ʃən/","def":"The use of many words when fewer would be clearer.","ex":"The official's circumlocution avoided a direct answer.","es":"circunloquio / rodeo","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'circum' (around) plus 'loqui' (to speak) - speaking in circles rather than to the point.","triv":"Dickens invented the 'Circumlocution Office' in Little Dorrit to satirize government departments that exist to avoid answering.","trap":"CIRCUM- with a U, then -LOCUTION.","esn":""},{"n":123,"w":"conscientious","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/ˌkɑːn.ʃiˈen.ʃəs/","def":"Careful to do work thoroughly and correctly.","ex":"A conscientious editor verifies every quotation.","es":"concienzudo / escrupuloso","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'conscientia' - knowledge shared within oneself, from 'com-' plus 'scire' (to know).","triv":"Conscience and science are the same root. Your conscience is what you know about yourself.","trap":"The word SCIENCE is hiding inside: con-SCIEN-tious. Then -TIOUS.","esn":""},{"n":124,"w":"demagogue","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/ˈdem.ə.ɡɑːɡ/","def":"A leader who gains support by appealing to emotion and prejudice.","ex":"The historian described how the demagogue exploited public fear.","es":"demagogo","org":"Greek","ety":"Greek 'demagogos', a leader of the people, from 'demos' (the people) plus 'agogos' (leading).","triv":"It was neutral in Athens - just 'popular leader'. Thucydides and the historians soured it permanently.","trap":"-OGUE ending, like catalogue and dialogue. The UE is silent.","esn":""},{"n":125,"w":"dichotomy","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/daɪˈkɑː.t̬ə.mi/","def":"A division into two sharply contrasting parts.","ex":"The essay questions the false dichotomy between art and science.","es":"dicotomía","org":"Greek","ety":"Greek 'dikha' (in two) plus 'temnein' (to cut) - a cutting in two.","triv":"That 'temnein' also gives us atom: 'a-tomos', the thing that cannot be cut. Then we split it anyway.","trap":"DI-CHOT-O-MY. CH says /k/.","esn":""},{"n":126,"w":"ebullient","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/ɪˈbʊl.jənt/","def":"Cheerful, energetic, and enthusiastic.","ex":"The winner gave an ebullient speech.","es":"exultante / efusivo","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'ebullire', to boil over, from 'ex-' plus 'bullire' - to boil, which also gives us 'boil' itself.","triv":"English has two words for bubbling enthusiasm - ebullient and effervescent - and both are literally about boiling liquid.","trap":"Double L. EB-ULL-IENT.","esn":""},{"n":127,"w":"equivocate","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/ɪˈkwɪv.ə.keɪt/","def":"To use unclear language to avoid committing to an answer.","ex":"The witness refused to equivocate during questioning.","es":"usar ambigüedades / evadir","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'aequus' (equal) plus 'vocare' (to call) - to call two different things by the same name.","triv":"To equivocate is not to lie. It is to choose a word that lets both meanings stand, so you can retreat later.","trap":"EQUI- like equal, then -VOCATE like vocal.","esn":""},{"n":128,"w":"exacerbate","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/ɪɡˈzæs.ɚ.beɪt/","def":"To make a problem or unpleasant situation worse.","ex":"Ignoring the leak will exacerbate the damage.","es":"agravar / empeorar","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'exacerbare', to irritate, from 'acerbus' - harsh, bitter, unripe.","triv":"Same bitter root as acerbic, acrid and acid. To exacerbate is to make something more sour.","trap":"EX-AC-ER-BATE. One C, and the middle is -CERB-, like an unripe 'sherbet'.","esn":""},{"n":129,"w":"fastidious","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/fæˈstɪd.i.əs/","def":"Very attentive to detail, accuracy, or cleanliness.","ex":"The fastidious researcher labeled every sample precisely.","es":"quisquilloso / meticuloso","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'fastidium', loathing or disgust - probably 'fastus' (arrogance) plus 'taedium' (weariness).","triv":"It meant 'easily disgusted' long before it meant 'careful about detail'. The insult is still faintly in there.","trap":"FAST plus -IDIOUS. The word 'fast' gets you started.","esn":""},{"n":130,"w":"grandiloquent","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/ɡrænˈdɪl.ə.kwənt/","def":"Using language that is elaborate and pompous.","ex":"The simple proposal was hidden beneath grandiloquent phrasing.","es":"grandilocuente","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'grandis' (grand) plus 'loqui' (to speak) - the same 'loqui' as in circumlocution, eloquent and ventriloquist.","triv":"A ventriloquist is a 'belly-speaker'. Latin was very literal about mouths.","trap":"-LOQUENT, not '-loquant'.","esn":""},{"n":131,"w":"harbinger","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/ˈhɑːr.bɪn.dʒɚ/","def":"A sign that something is approaching.","ex":"The first robin was a harbinger of spring.","es":"presagio / precursor","org":"French","ety":"Old French 'herbergere', one who provides lodging, from a Germanic word for army-shelter. A harbinger rode ahead of the royal party to arrange rooms.","triv":"The advance scout became the sign of what is coming. Same root as 'harbor' - a shelter.","trap":"The N is intrusive, added like the N in messenger and passenger. HAR-BIN-GER.","esn":""},{"n":132,"w":"iconoclast","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/aɪˈkɑː.nə.klæst/","def":"A person who challenges established beliefs or traditions.","ex":"The artist became known as an iconoclast.","es":"iconoclasta","org":"Greek","ety":"Greek 'eikon' (image) plus 'klastes' (breaker) - an image-breaker.","triv":"Literal history: in 8th-century Byzantium, factions physically smashed religious icons. Now it just means someone who attacks cherished beliefs.","trap":"ICON plus O plus CLAST. Build it from 'icon'.","esn":""},{"n":133,"w":"inchoate","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/ɪnˈkoʊ.ət/","def":"Only partly formed or not yet fully developed.","ex":"The committee shaped the inchoate idea into a clear plan.","es":"incipiente / rudimentario","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'incohare', to begin - originally a farming term, thought to refer to hitching an ox to a plow to start the furrow.","triv":"The beginning of everything, in Latin, was a harness and a field.","trap":"Said in-KOH-it, with a K sound and no stress on the end. The spelling tempts you to say 'in-CHOH-ate'.","esn":""},{"n":134,"w":"juxtaposition","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/ˌdʒʌk.stə.pəˈzɪʃ.ən/","def":"The placement of contrasting things side by side.","ex":"The photograph's juxtaposition of old and new was striking.","es":"yuxtaposición","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'juxta' (next to) plus French 'position'. A placing side by side.","triv":"A rare Latin-French hybrid, coined in the 1600s because English had no single word for 'put these two things next to each other'.","trap":"JUXTA- with an X and no C.","esn":"'Yuxtaposición' empieza con Y; el inglés con J. Lo demás es igual."},{"n":135,"w":"kaleidoscope","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/kəˈlaɪ.də.skoʊp/","def":"A changing pattern or mixture of colors, shapes, or events.","ex":"The market offered a kaleidoscope of colors and aromas.","es":"caleidoscopio","org":"Greek","ety":"Coined in 1817 by the Scottish physicist David Brewster from Greek 'kalos' (beautiful) plus 'eidos' (form) plus 'skopein' (to look at) - a beautiful-form watcher.","triv":"Brewster invented the toy and the word on the same day. It is one of the few words in the list with a known birthday.","trap":"KALEIDO-, with EI. Three Greek blocks: kal + eido + scope.","esn":""},{"n":136,"w":"lachrymose","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/ˈlæk.rə.moʊs/","def":"Tearful or likely to cause sadness.","ex":"The film's lachrymose ending moved the audience.","es":"lloroso / lacrimógeno","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'lacrima', a tear. The -CHRY- spelling is a medieval flourish; classical Latin wrote 'lacrima'.","triv":"Scribes added the H and the Y to make the word look more Greek and therefore more learned. Pure showing off, preserved forever.","trap":"LACH-RY-MOSE. The CH and the Y are both decorative mistakes you must copy exactly.","esn":"'Lágrima' es la misma raíz. Pero el inglés escribe -CHRY- en vez de -GRI-."},{"n":137,"w":"magnanimous","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/mæɡˈnæn.ə.məs/","def":"Generous and forgiving, especially toward a rival.","ex":"The champion was magnanimous in victory.","es":"magnánimo / generoso","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'magnus' (great) plus 'animus' (soul, spirit) - great-souled.","triv":"Aristotle's term for the ideal character was 'megalopsychos' - Greek for exactly the same thing. Latin translated it word for word.","trap":"MAGN- then -ANIMOUS. The word 'animus' is inside.","esn":""},{"n":138,"w":"metamorphosis","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/ˌmet̬.əˈmɔːr.fə.sɪs/","def":"A complete transformation in form or character.","ex":"The caterpillar's metamorphosis produces a butterfly.","es":"metamorfosis","org":"Greek","ety":"Greek 'meta-' (change) plus 'morphe' (form) - a change of shape.","triv":"Ovid's poem of that name, written around 8 AD, is fifteen books of people turning into trees, birds and rivers.","trap":"-PHOSIS at the end, with PH. The stress lands on MOR.","esn":""},{"n":139,"w":"obfuscate","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/ˈɑːb.fə.skeɪt/","def":"To make something unclear or difficult to understand.","ex":"Technical jargon can obfuscate a simple explanation.","es":"ofuscar / oscurecer","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'obfuscare', to darken, from 'ob-' plus 'fuscus' - dark, dusky.","triv":"To obfuscate is to throw shade, quite literally. The word is deliberately harder than 'confuse', which is itself a small joke.","trap":"OB-FUS-CATE. English keeps the B that Spanish drops.","esn":"'Ofuscar' no lleva B. El inglés sí: OBfuscate."},{"n":140,"w":"paradigm","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/ˈper.ə.daɪm/","def":"A typical model, pattern, or way of thinking.","ex":"The discovery created a new paradigm in medicine.","es":"paradigma / modelo","org":"Greek","ety":"Greek 'paradeigma', a pattern or model, from 'para-' (beside) plus 'deiknynai' - to show.","triv":"The G is silent here but comes roaring back in 'paradigmatic'. English hides letters until it needs them.","trap":"Silent G before the M: -DIGM. Same trick as 'diaphragm' and 'phlegm'.","esn":""},{"n":141,"w":"philanthropy","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/fɪˈlæn.θrə.pi/","def":"The act of promoting human welfare through generous giving.","ex":"Her philanthropy funded scholarships for rural students.","es":"filantropía","org":"Greek","ety":"Greek 'philos' (loving) plus 'anthropos' (human being) - love of humankind.","triv":"Its opposite, misanthropy, uses 'misein' - to hate. Same 'anthropos' as anthropology.","trap":"PHIL- with PH, then -ANTHROPY.","esn":"'Filantropía' usa F. El inglés usa PH para el mismo sonido griego."},{"n":142,"w":"prestidigitation","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/ˌpres.tɪˌdɪdʒ.əˈteɪ.ʃən/","def":"Skillful hand movements used to perform magic tricks.","ex":"The magician's prestidigitation amazed the children.","es":"prestidigitación / juegos de manos","org":"French","ety":"French, from 'preste' (nimble, quick - the same word as Italian 'presto') plus Latin 'digitus' (finger). Nimble-fingered.","triv":"Coined in the 1800s to give stage magic a respectable scientific-sounding name. When a magician says 'presto', that is this word's front half.","trap":"Six syllables. Chunk it: PRES-TI-DIG-I-TA-TION. 'Digit' sits in the middle.","esn":""},{"n":143,"w":"prognosticate","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/prɑːɡˈnɑː.stə.keɪt/","def":"To predict a future event or outcome.","ex":"Experts hesitate to prognosticate during uncertain conditions.","es":"pronosticar / predecir","org":"Greek","ety":"Greek 'pro-' (before) plus 'gignoskein' (to know) - to know in advance.","triv":"Same 'gnosis' as diagnosis, agnostic and the Spanish 'conocer'. Knowing runs deep in Indo-European.","trap":"PROGNOS- keeps the G that you can barely hear.","esn":"'Pronosticar' no lleva G. El inglés sí: proGnosticate."},{"n":144,"w":"pulchritudinous","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/ˌpʌl.krəˈtuː.də.nəs/","def":"Physically beautiful.","ex":"The poet described the pulchritudinous landscape.","es":"bellísimo / de gran belleza","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'pulcher' - beautiful, plus the suffix -tudo forming abstract nouns.","triv":"Famous as the ugliest word in English for the most beautiful idea. It sounds like a throat condition and means 'gorgeous'.","trap":"PULCHRI- with CHR, then -TUDINOUS. Five syllables of pure Latin.","esn":""},{"n":145,"w":"recalcitrant","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/rɪˈkæl.sə.trənt/","def":"Stubbornly resistant to authority or control.","ex":"The recalcitrant mule refused to move.","es":"recalcitrante / terco","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'recalcitrare', to kick back, from 're-' plus 'calx, calcis' - the heel.","triv":"The image is a mule planting its heels and kicking. Same 'calx' as in 'calcitrate' and, distantly, the anatomy of the heel bone.","trap":"RE-CAL-CI-TRANT. One L, then CI.","esn":""},{"n":146,"w":"sagacious","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/səˈɡeɪ.ʃəs/","def":"Having sound judgment and practical wisdom.","ex":"The sagacious mentor offered timely advice.","es":"sagaz / perspicaz","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'sagax', of keen perception - originally used of a hunting dog's sense of smell.","triv":"Wisdom, in Latin, started as a good nose. Related to 'presage' and to the English verb 'seek'.","trap":"-ACIOUS, matching perspicacious and audacious.","esn":""},{"n":147,"w":"surreptitious","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/ˌsɝː.əpˈtɪʃ.əs/","def":"Kept secret because it would not be approved.","ex":"They exchanged a surreptitious glance during the meeting.","es":"subrepticio / clandestino","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'surripere', to snatch away secretly, from 'sub-' (under) plus 'rapere' (to seize) - the same 'rapere' as rapid and rapture.","triv":"The 'sub-' assimilated into SUR-, which is why there are two Rs where you would not expect them.","trap":"Double R, then -EPTITIOUS. Not 'surrepticious'.","esn":""},{"n":148,"w":"ubiquitous","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/juːˈbɪk.wə.t̬əs/","def":"Present or appearing everywhere.","ex":"Smartphones have become ubiquitous in modern life.","es":"ubicuo / omnipresente","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'ubique' - everywhere, from 'ubi', where.","triv":"The 'ubi' is the same one in the Spanish verb 'ubicar', to locate. Ubiquity is being in every 'where' at once.","trap":"UBI-QUI-TOUS. Two QU-style chunks, then -TOUS.","esn":"'Ubicuo' viene de la misma raíz que 'ubicar'. Está en todas partes."},{"n":149,"w":"vicissitude","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/vɪˈsɪs.ə.tuːd/","def":"A change in circumstances, often an unwelcome one.","ex":"The memoir describes every vicissitude of the long voyage.","es":"vicisitud / cambio de fortuna","org":"Latin","ety":"Latin 'vicissitudo', change, from 'vicis' - a turn or change of circumstance.","triv":"That 'vicis' is the 'vice' in vice-president (one who serves in another's turn) and in 'vice versa' - the turn having been reversed.","trap":"Double S in the middle, one C: vi-CI-SS-itude.","esn":"'Vicisitud' lleva una S. El inglés dobla: viciSSitude."},{"n":150,"w":"zeitgeist","lvl":"Advanced","ipa":"/ˈtsaɪt.ɡaɪst/","def":"The defining ideas and spirit of a particular period.","ex":"The exhibition captures the cultural zeitgeist of the decade.","es":"espíritu de la época","org":"German","ety":"German 'Zeit' (time) plus 'Geist' (spirit) - the spirit of the age. Borrowed whole, capital letter and all.","triv":"A gift from German Romantic philosophy, popularized in English by Matthew Arnold in the 1800s. German nouns are always capitalized, which is why you often still see Zeitgeist with a Z.","trap":"German EI says /aɪ/ - 'TSYTE-guyste'. Both halves use EI, never IE.","esn":""}];
